// Copilot review (PR #2516), comment 4113449699: every existing thin-link
// f64-tuple-array gate either calls a lowerer PROBE entry point directly
// (--probe-filtered-body-reset-boundary etc. in main.sio) or runs a single
// file with no cross-module import at all -- so none of them exercises the
// real path module_loader.sio's thin_build_compiled_unit builds: an IMPORTED
// callee whose return type is a tuple with an f64 array slot, going through
// the actual multi-module frontend (--native-compile), reset/collected at
// its real per-UNIT boundary rather than a synthetic one a probe sets up by
// hand. `make_pair`'s second slot is that array; `main.sio` imports it with
// an ordinary `use`.
//
// `helper` below is private and non-exported, and ALSO returns a tuple with
// an f64 array slot. `tta_other.sio` (loaded as a separate compiled unit of
// the same program) declares its own private `helper` with a different,
// non-tuple signature. Same name, two units: thin_build_compiled_unit resets
// and re-collects LOWER_FN_TUPLE_ARR_* once per unit (see its own comment),
// so this pins that one unit's collected mask for a name never survives
// into, or corrupts, another unit's collection for a same-named-but-different
// function -- consecutive units reusing a function name is the other half of
// what this gate was asked to cover.
fn helper() -> (f64, [f64; 2]) with Mut, Panic, Div, Alloc {
    (9.5, [90.5, 91.5])
}

pub fn make_pair() -> (f64, [f64; 3]) with Mut, Panic, Div, Alloc {
    (1.5, [10.5, 20.5, 30.5])
}
